2026-10-24T22:26:07.4734960Z    image gate: coleman_ames_house_1811_prairie chicagology_photo: verdict "differs", 3 difference(s) open: the basement lights' grilles: the photograph's are scrolled ironwork round a central rosette; the build's read as plain vertical bars from here; the south flank (T-3079, ruled; T-3099 to settle): past the front block the photograph's flank is short. The coped south wall runs back from the tall stack to x 1160 px, the wall head there drops to a lower brick range whose end stands at x 1197 px, and past it a separate pavilion with an arched door (about u -16, v 9 through this camera, by its foot at y 1215 px) stands in the sheet's rear section with lawn to the right of it. Through this camera the sheet's straight south wall reaches x 1312 px at u -18.4 with a three-storey range all the way. Its windows differ with it: arched ground-floor heads at x 1143, 1163, 1180 and 1195 px and tall first-floor lights over them, where the build has flat-headed sash at its own spacing. The photograph is of about 1888 and the scene is 1904, so this is not copied: what stood in 1904 is T-3099's; the front block's coped south wall: the photograph's coping, read against the flank's vanishing point, stands about 0.6 m higher than the build's level 10.95 m and runs back to about x 1160 px, about 2.8 m past the front block's rear wall, before it drops; both belong to the flank's question (T-3099) and are read with it
2026-10-24T22:26:07.4750761Z    image gate: coleman_ames_house_1811_prairie glessner_blog_print: verdict "differs", 2 difference(s) open: the porch arcade steps down to the south on the plate: its bases by 0, 4 and 9 px (y 722, 726 and 731) and its crowns by 0, 6 and 12 px (y 646, 652 and 658), where the build stands level (bases y 720, crowns y 646-647). Going round the 0.9 m round lowers a crown by under 1 px through T-2541's camera, so the curve does not explain it. A camera placed further out (T-3079) changes how depth along the south flank projects, so it is that re-solve's question first. T-3079 kept T-2541's camera (the Chicagology photograph's own solve agrees with a near eye), so the step-down is no longer waiting on a re-solve: it is the arcade's own question again; the south flank's depth: between the stone face and the step at x 573 px the plate draws six windows a storey (centres about x 525, 541, 553, 564, 571 and 576 px), and past x 584 sky over a lower pavilion (the coach house, its arched door on the Chicagology print); the build's six flank bays render at x 555-611 px and its range runs on to x 616 px (y 589) and its rear section to x 624, where the plate shows sky. The camera's distance (about 15 m, fixed by the canted bay face's 1.1 m depth) puts the flank about 2.6 times too long: a camera about 32 m out puts the bays at x 529-574 px and the step on the sheet's '3B'/'2B' wall (T-3079). T-3079 RULED (2026-10-24): the camera stands and the flank is the question. The Chicagology photograph (chicagology_photo.json), a different negative from a spot about 3.5 m further out, solves independently to a near camera that fits the front, the canted end, the bay's foot, the curb and the parkway tree within 3.4 px, and misses the same flank the same way (its far end 118 px short of the sheet's straight south wall at u -18.4); freed to fit the flank, either camera walks out to 32-47 m and loses the canted end and the ground by 46-63 px. Both pictures, of about 1888, show a shorter range and, past it, a separate pavilion with an arched door; the 1911 sheet, 23 years on, draws one building. What stood on 1 July 1904 is T-3099's to find
